A GLOBAL struct is a struct too.

    char_pointer_type = char_type;          tccgen.c:392

Both file-scope. micro-c compiled that to a struct copy whose SOURCE address
was char_type's first eight bytes -- the integer 3 -- so it read from address
3. tcc dies there, at marker N07, immediately after preprocess_start returns.

WHERE IT CAME FROM. primary_expr_variable decides whether to load a variable's
value:

    int size = register_size;
    if(options == TLO_LOCAL || options == TLO_ARGUMENT)
        size = current_target->size;
    if(register_size >= size)                  <- "do not load a struct"
        emit_out(load_value(size, ...));

`size` only becomes the type's real width for a LOCAL or an ARGUMENT. For a
GLOBAL or STATIC it stays at register_size, so the guard could not see that
the thing was a struct at all. The local form was fixed when tccgen.c:909 hit
it; the global form is the same rule in the other storage class.

THE FIRST ATTEMPT WAS WRONG AND THE SUITE CAUGHT IT. Suppressing the load
whenever the type is bigger than a register broke cases 31 and 43, because
that load does DOUBLE DUTY: for a scalar it fetches the value, and for a
GLOBAL ARRAY it dereferences the pointer cell the symbol actually holds.
`static struct Big items[4]` has current_target->size 48 and still needs it.
array_modifier separates the two jobs, so the suppression now applies only to
a non-array variable.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX. Struct assignment is ALSO broken on aarch64, for both
storage classes, and that is a separate fault: the emitted copy loop is
instruction-for-instruction identical to amd64's, which passes. Case 05 stays
red on aarch64 and is not addressed here.

    amd64    43 pass  0 fail  2 known gaps   (was 41 pass 2 fail)
    aarch64  struct assignment still fails -- bug 2, untouched

diff --git a/cc_core.c b/cc_core.c
index 3520856..38eadd3 100644
--- a/cc_core.c
+++ b/cc_core.c
@@ -1355,11 +1355,34 @@ void primary_expr_variable(void)
 		{
 			size = current_target->size;
 		}
-		/* A LOCAL STRUCT IS NOT LOADED EITHER. Same rule as the dereference
-		 * sites and member access: a struct is used by ADDRESS. Without this,
+		/* A STRUCT IS NOT LOADED EITHER. Same rule as the dereference sites
+		 * and member access: a struct is used by ADDRESS. Without this,
 		 * `vtop[0] = tmp` with a local struct tmp tried load_value(16) --
-		 * tccgen.c:909, where tcc swaps two SValues through a local. */
-		if(register_size >= size)
+		 * tccgen.c:909, where tcc swaps two SValues through a local.
+		 *
+		 * AND THE GUARD HAS TO ASK THE TYPE, NOT `size`. size only becomes the
+		 * type's real width for TLO_LOCAL and TLO_ARGUMENT above; for a GLOBAL
+		 * or STATIC it stays at register_size, so the test could not see that
+		 * the thing was a struct at all and loaded its first eight bytes as if
+		 * they were the value. tcc hits it immediately:
+		 *     char_pointer_type = char_type;          tccgen.c:392
+		 * both file-scope, so the source of the copy became char_type.t -- the
+		 * integer 3 -- used as an address.
+		 *
+		 * The local form was fixed and the global form is the same rule in the
+		 * other storage class, which is the fourth time that sentence appears
+		 * in this file.
+		 *
+		 * AND NOT FOR AN ARRAY. This load does double duty: for a scalar it
+		 * fetches the value, and for a GLOBAL ARRAY it dereferences the
+		 * pointer cell the symbol actually holds. Suppressing it on element
+		 * size alone killed the second job -- `static struct Big items[4]`
+		 * has current_target->size 48 and still needs the load. array_modifier
+		 * separates them.
+		 */
+		int suppress_struct_load = (register_size < current_target->size)
+		                           && (type->array_modifier <= 1);
+		if(register_size >= size && !suppress_struct_load)
 		{
 			emit_out(load_value(size, current_target->is_signed));
 		}
